# Firefox add-ons (webextensions/README.md): Firefox's WebExtensions APIs that
# Chromium lacks. The functions are in dubna's src/extensions/; this registers them
# in their namespaces' schemas, builds them and registers their prefs. Firefox's
# names for Chromium's APIs are aliases: browser.menus and the "menus" permission
# are contextMenus. A Firefox add-on's Manifest V3 background page (background.scripts
# or .page) runs as Manifest V2's does, unless the manifest also has a service worker;
# runtime.getContexts() lists such a page as the background.
# A Firefox add-on's version Chromium refuses ("3.4resigned1", "01", "1.2.3.4.5",
# ".") loads as its leading numbers, with the full string as the version name,
# and a name longer than 75 characters is cut to 75 (firefox_version.cc).
# A Firefox add-on's background with "type": "module" loads its scripts as modules.
# Locale files load as Firefox reads them: a message of any name ("first-install-body"),
# a placeholder the message doesn't define becomes empty, and homepage_url
# is localized too, also in a packed install, which reads the manifest once
# before it localizes it.
# declarative_net_request without its permission loads without its rulesets,
# as in Firefox.
# A Firefox add-on whose manifest names an icon or a side panel it doesn't have
# loads, with Chromium's error as a warning, and a path with spaces around it
# (" icon.png") reads as one without, as in Firefox (firefox_file_paths.h).
# A tab that hasn't committed a navigation has the URL about:blank for a Firefox
# add-on (firefox_add_on.h), as in Firefox.
# Shortcuts load as Firefox reads them: Ctrl+Alt, F1 to F12 alone or with Shift,
# MacCtrl as Ctrl, any number of shortcuts, and a command without a description
# or a key for this platform; the shortcuts page takes them too (function_keys.cc).
# A Manifest V2 event page that asks for webRequest runs as a persistent page.
# A Firefox add-on's page_action next to a browser_action or action, or in Manifest V3, is a
# second action that pageAction.* reaches (firefox_page_action.h).
# A Firefox add-on's page action shows on the pages that its show_matches and
# hide_matches match, until show() or hide() for the tab
# (firefox_show_matches.h): TabHelper makes each tab's matcher, GetIsVisible()
# reads its result under show() and hide(), which a tab keeps also when it
# equals the match.
# menus.create and update take Firefox's icons (not shown) and its password,
# bookmark and tools_menu contexts (the item shows in its other contexts).
# browserSettings is Firefox's, read-only: each setting is a types.ChromeSetting,
# and the preference functions hand its key to firefox_browser_settings.cc.
# So is privacy.network.peerConnectionEnabled, which is always true.
# privacy.network.webRTCIPHandlingPolicy takes Firefox's proxy_only, which the
# renderer takes as disable_non_proxied_udp, Chromium's strictest policy.
# storage.session is there in Manifest V2 Firefox add-ons too, as in Firefox.
# So is scripting.
# A Firefox add-on's storage.session counts Firefox's sizes
# (firefox_session_size.h) and isn't in its content scripts, and its
# storage.*.get() reads a key whose default is undefined (firefox_storage.h).
# Firefox's theme API, read-only: getCurrent() and onUpdated give the browser
# theme's colours in Firefox's key names (theme_api.cc), to any Firefox add-on.
# browserAction has action's setBadgeTextColor, getBadgeTextColor and
# getUserSettings, as in Firefox (firefox_browser_action.h).
# browserAction's and action's setters and getters take Firefox's windowId: a
# value for the tabs of one window (firefox_action_window.h).
# contentScripts.register() is Firefox's (firefox_content_scripts.cc): dynamic
# content scripts, with code as well as files, that the scripting API doesn't
# list or remove. Chromium's contentScripts schema, which only types the
# content_scripts manifest key, is left out of the schemas the bindings read.
# proxy.onRequest and proxy.onError are Firefox's (proxy_internal_api.h): while an
# add-on listens, ProxyConfigMonitor's settings are a PAC script that
# ChromeMojoProxyResolverFactory answers by asking the add-on
# (firefox_proxy_config.h); proxy_custom_bindings.js reports its answer.
# setIcon rejects on the browser's error for a Firefox add-on, as in Firefox
# (set_icon.js); other extensions keep Chromium's promise, which resolves.
# A same-document navigation to about:blank has its document's origin in its
# UrlInfo, not the initiator's, so an add-on's tabs.update() to about:blank#ref
# on a new tab passes navigation_request.cc's and navigator.cc's site checks.
# privacy.network.networkPredictionEnabled set to true turns prediction on: it
# wrote kDefault, which the upstreams make kDisabled.
# A Firefox add-on's menus item without contexts shows in all of them, or in
# its parent's; a run of its radio items may have none checked; and its
# targetUrlPatterns apply whatever its contexts (firefox_menu_items.h).
# A Firefox add-on's menus item has at most one onclick, the last page's to set
# it, and removing the item from any page drops it (firefox_menu_items_onclick.h).
# A click on a Firefox add-on's item with a command (menus.create's command)
# runs its toolbar action, as the shortcut does, instead of firing onClicked
# (firefox_menu_clicks.h).
# A Firefox add-on's OnClickData has Firefox's modifiers, button and linkText,
# and a middle click runs its item in a page's menu, as in Firefox
# (firefox_menu_model_adapter.h).
# Android's tabs get Firefox's user agent on addons.mozilla.org too
# (firefox_amo_user_agent.h), after the desktop site's observer, whose
# override comes back off AMO.
# A packed install of a Firefox add-on doesn't decode its SVG icons, which
# failed the install (firefox_svg_icons.h), so the browser's ImageLoader
# doesn't decode an .svg file either. Its background page draws its SVG
# action icons and setIcon() draws an SVG path at the icon's sizes
# (firefox_svg_icons.js, firefox_manifest_svg_icons.h). The drawn icon is the
# action's default icon, under any icon the add-on sets, for a page action
# too (firefox_svg_default_icon.h, FirefoxActionValues::SvgDefaultIcon()).
# On Android, an extension that fails to install, such as a .xpi with a broken
# manifest, says why in a message on the tab, as desktop's infobar does, and
# without an active window to tell, the install UI's tab is the one the user
# sees, not the first tab (install_failure_message_android.h).
# A downloaded .xpi without a Gecko ID installs with the ID of its AMO
# signature's certificate, as in Firefox (firefox_xpi_signature.h).
# A packed install of a Firefox add-on decodes its images with shrink_to_fit,
# so an icon too large for the decoder's limit is halved until it fits and
# saved at that size, as Firefox installs it; other extensions still fail.
# A Firefox add-on's tabs.onUpdated status follows the tab's top frame, as in
# Firefox: "loading" before the URL too, and nothing for a frame's navigation
# (firefox_tab_status.h). Other extensions keep Chromium's status updates.
# A pinned tab that closes sends a Firefox add-on no tabs.onUpdated "pinned:
# false", as in Firefox (firefox_closing_tab.h).
# A Firefox add-on's @@ui_locale is Firefox's, the BCP 47 tag that
# getUILanguage() gives, with or without _locales, in getMessage() and in
# its CSS (firefox_ui_locale.h).
# i18n.getPreferredSystemLanguages() is Firefox's: the system's languages, read
# in the browser, so content scripts have it too (firefox_i18n_api.h).
# tabs.create takes Firefox's discarded and title: the tab is inserted unloaded,
# as a session restore's tab that waits to be selected, and a Firefox add-on
# calls such a tab discarded (firefox_discarded_tab.h).
# before it localizes it. A Firefox add-on's default_locale folder can have
# any name ("jp"), placeholders that Chromium refused load as Firefox reads
# them (firefox_locale_files.h), and i18n.getMessage() puts in substitutions
# as Firefox does (firefox_i18n_substitutions.h).
# runtime.getDocumentId() is Firefox's, synchronous: the renderer finds the
# frame of a window (blink's WebFrame::FromWindowOrNullopt()) or frame
# element and asks the browser for its documentId with a synchronous message
# (firefox_document_id.h).
# A Firefox add-on gets each documentId in Firefox's form, a UUID, and may
# send it back in either form: the renderer turns it at the bindings'
# results, events and requests, and at messaging's sender and options
# (firefox_document_ids.h).
# UserScriptLoader::SendUpdate() sends a renderer process only the
# contentScripts scripts of its cookie store (firefox_content_script_stores.h).
# A Firefox add-on's targetUrlPatterns take any scheme and match as Firefox's
# (firefox_menu_items.h); a link with an invalid URL is a link, whose href
# Blink sends apart (invalid_link_url); and its item titles show in full.
# Blink sends the text selected in the frame for every menu
# (firefox_selected_text.h), which gives a Firefox add-on's items the selection
# context next to the selection, and its titles' %s and selectionText, as in
# Firefox (ContextMenuMatcher::ItemTitle()).
# A downloaded .xpi installs only with a valid AMO signature, whose
# certificate's common name is its ID, as in Firefox; one without a Gecko ID
# installs with that ID (firefox_xpi_signature.h). ZipFileInstaller's filter
# is public for the check, to which a file it doesn't unzip isn't missing,
# and a theme's META-INF/ is unzipped too, for its signature.
# An unsigned .xpi installs after a warning, first in the install prompt's
# list, and chrome://extensions marks it as not verified among its runtime
# warnings (firefox_xpi_installer.h).
# menus.onShown, onHidden and refresh() are Firefox's: a page's, an action's
# and a tab's menu tell their ContextMenuMatcher where they appended the
# extension items and when they open and close (firefox_menu_events.h).
# A button menu built or shown under a ScopedFirefoxMenuAction is that
# action's: a page action's own button shows its page_action items
# (firefox_tab_menus.h).
# Firefox's onShown listener gets an undefined tab where the menu has none
# (firefox_menu_shown_tab.h).
# What the climb adds to the API schemas is marked "firefox_only", and a relaxed
# requirement has its "chromium" value: only a Firefox add-on's bindings read the
# schemas as they are, every other extension's read their Chromium view, and lose
# the marked members and result properties (firefox_schema_view.h,
# firefox_add_on_bindings.h). A Firefox namespace's feature takes Chromium's
# delegated availability check (firefox_add_on_availability.h).
# A Firefox add-on's action menu starts with its items and a separator, as in
# Firefox; past six items, the sixth place is a submenu with the add-on's name
# that holds the rest, which Chromium dropped (firefox_menu_items.cc). A click
# there has the active tab's URL as its pageUrl (firefox_menu_events.cc).
# menus.create and update take Firefox's icons, which the item shows
# (firefox_menu_icons.h), and its password, bookmark and tools_menu contexts;
# a password item shows on a password field, a bookmark or tools_menu one in
# its other contexts.
# menus.create and update take Firefox's viewTypes: a Firefox add-on's item
# with them shows only in the menus of a page in those views, so in no tab's
# or toolbar button's menu, and onClicked's info has the page's viewType, as
# onShown's has (firefox_menu_view_types.h).
# Bookmark nodes have Firefox's type, and bookmarks.create takes it
# (firefox_bookmark_nodes.h).
# The bookmarks API gives a Firefox add-on Firefox's folder IDs, such as
# toolbar_____ and unfiled_____, in its nodes and events (firefox_bookmark_ids.h).
# The menus items of a Firefox add-on whose background Firefox runs as an event
# page, Manifest V3's or one with "persistent": false, stay across a restart,
# a disable and a reinstall of the same version, in its extension prefs, and
# such an add-on's items need an id, as in Firefox (firefox_menu_store.h).
# A tab's menu and an action's menu match a Firefox add-on's
# documentUrlPatterns against the tab's URL as the menu opens, and the tab's
# menu lists Firefox add-ons in the order they were installed
# (firefox_tab_menus.h).
# A Firefox add-on's menus items get Firefox's access keys: its title's & as
# Firefox reads it, else the label's first letter or digit, else a digit
# (firefox_menu_access_keys.h).
# menus.getTargetElement() is Firefox's: Blink keeps each window's last menu
# target with a new ID (firefox_menu_target.h), which the menu's params carry
# (firefox_target_element_id) into onShown's and onClicked's targetElementId,
# and blink's WebElement::FirefoxMenuTarget() gives the element back to the
# window's pages and content scripts. Only a Firefox add-on with the menus
# permission gets them, and its content scripts get browser.menus with
# getTargetElement() alone (firefox_menu_target_element.h).
# Where Firefox refuses a menus.create or update as Chromium does, a Firefox
# add-on gets Firefox's text: an id in use or missing, an item made its own or
# its descendant's child, an invalid url pattern (firefox_menu_errors.h). Its
# runtime.lastError is null without an error, as in Firefox
# (firefox_last_error.h).
# A Firefox add-on's host patterns in optional_permissions are optional host
# permissions in Manifest V3 too, as in Firefox (firefox_host_permissions.h).
# An unsigned .xpi's install is checked again when it happens, after the
# prompt: CrxInstaller runs a Firefox add-on's install check just before it
# adds the extension (firefox_xpi_installer.h).
# pageAction.openPopup() and the menus command _execute_page_action run a
# Firefox add-on's page action through the window's page action buttons
# (firefox_page_action_buttons.h), which the desktop browser registers.
